{
  "rationale": "This is iteration 0, so there is no race telemetry or integration feedback yet. The geometry is a conservative baseline built from the contract limits. Five line sensors at 12 mm pitch (x = +0.024 to -0.024, y = 0.06) cover a 48 mm lateral span, about twice the 25 mm tape width. Each sensor footprint is 12 mm, so the array has no blind gaps across the line. Placing the sensors 0.06 m ahead of the axle gives the Integrator look-ahead to react before the robot reaches a bend. The wheel track is 0.12 m, a middle value that gives usable turn authority without making the robot sluggish. The wheel radius is 0.03 m, which keeps wheel speed reasonable for the roughly 1.5 m/s average speed needed to finish in 3 s. The mass is 0.6 kg, a light body that helps acceleration and yaw response. The body width of 0.10 m is not a lever: the off-track limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), and at this width the 0.06 m floor governs, so width only matters if it is much larger. Body length is 0.16 m, which has no direct effect on dynamics. Expected result: a completing run on the oval and s-bend tracks, with the main failure modes (line loss on tight slalom bends, or overshoot) visible in the measurements requested below. Hypotheses to test: H1, a wider sensor span gives smoother steering on large bends; H2, a 0.12 m track gives enough turn authority for slalom without oscillation; H3, sensors further ahead (y near 0.08) reduce line-loss events on sharp bends at the cost of a noisier signal.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.024, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": 0.012, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": -0.012, "y": 0.06},
      {"x": -0.024, "y": 0.06}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.10,
    "body_length": 0.16,
    "mass": 0.6
  },
  "intent": "Establish a balanced baseline: a five-sensor, 12 mm pitch array with continuous coverage across the line, sensors 0.06 m ahead of the axle for look-ahead, and a light, medium-track body. This gives the Integrator a stable platform on which to tune weights and gains. Next iteration, adjust sensor span and forward offset based on which track segments produce line loss or oscillation.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "controller_errors", "noise_robustness"],
    "questions": [
      "Does the robot finish the oval, s-bend and slalom tracks, and where does progress stop when it does not?",
      "Where on each track do line-loss events occur: on tight bends, at track entry, or on exits? Report the approximate track location if you can.",
      "Does the 0.12 m wheel track give enough turn authority on slalom, or does it saturate (turn limited by ±4095)?",
      "Is there evidence the sensor span (±0.024 m) is too narrow or too wide: for example, signal saturating at ±max weight, or readings all zero on bends?",
      "Does the mass of 0.6 kg cause visible overshoot or slow speed build-up?"
    ]
  },
  "learning": "No prior race data. This is a baseline with no evidence yet; all hypotheses H1 to H3 remain untested and should be checked against the first race's line-loss, rms_error and steering_oscillation values.",
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Baseline 5-sensor body",
      "notes": "First geometry: five 12 mm sensors at 12 mm pitch, 0.06 m ahead of the axle, 0.12 m track, 0.6 kg body. No race evidence yet, so it is a contract-limit baseline built to be measured and revised."
    }
  }
}
